# Skill: Read GPU Hardware Telemetry on Windows (NVIDIA / AMD / Intel / Fallback)

**Standard American English**
**Scope:** How to obtain GPU temperature, VRAM usage, GPU utilization, power, fan speed, and clocks on Windows, then forward to VRChat OSC.

---

## 1. Mental Model

Windows does **not** have one API like `GetGpuStats()`.
Each vendor exposes telemetry through its own management stack:

| Vendor | Primary API | Language / Binding | Best For |
|--------|-------------|--------------------|----------|
| NVIDIA | NVML (`nvml.dll`) | C/C++, Python `pynvml` | Temp, VRAM, GPU util, power, fan, clocks |
| AMD | ADLX SDK | C++, C# wrappers | Temp, VRAM, GPU activity, FPS, fan, power |
| Intel | oneAPI Level Zero SysMan (ZES) | C/C++, Python bindings | Temp, power, frequency, engine activity |
| Any | LibreHardwareMonitorLib | C# / .NET | Cross-vendor fallback for temp/load/VRAM |
| Any | DXGI `QueryVideoMemoryInfo` | C++ / C# | Local + non-local GPU memory budget/usage |

Rule: detect GPU vendor from `DXGI` / `WMI`, then dispatch to the correct backend.

---

## 2. NVIDIA — NVML via `pynvml`

### Call Chain

nvidia-smi

└─ nvml.dll

└─ pynvml

├─ nvmlInit()

├─ nvmlDeviceGetHandleByIndex(i)

├─ nvmlDeviceGetTemperature()

├─ nvmlDeviceGetMemoryInfo()

├─ nvmlDeviceGetUtilizationRates()

├─ nvmlDeviceGetPowerUsage()

├─ nvmlDeviceGetFanSpeed()

└─ nvmlShutdown()


### Example Input

python

import pynvml  # pip install nvidia-ml-py

pynvml.nvmlInit()

handle = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetHandleByIndex(0)

temp_c      = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetTemperature(handle, pynvml.NVML_TEMPERATURE_GPU)

mem         = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetMemoryInfo(handle)

util        = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetUtilizationRates(handle)

power_w     = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetPowerUsage(handle) / 1000.0

fan_pct     = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetFanSpeed(handle)


### Expected Effect
Reads live NVIDIA GPU telemetry from the driver without spawning `nvidia-smi`.

### Example Output Values

text

temp_c      = 71

mem.used    = 5500000000   # bytes

mem.total   = 8589934592   # bytes

util.gpu    = 83           # percent

power_w     = 212.4

fan_pct     = 47


---

## 3. AMD — ADLX SDK

### Call Chain

AMD driver

└─ ADLX runtime (ships with driver)

└─ ADLX Performance Monitoring

├─ IADLXSystemServices

├─ IADLXGPUList

├─ IADLXGPU

├─ IADLXPerformanceMonitoringServices

└─ GetCurrentGpuMetrics()

├─ GPU temperature

├─ GPU activity %

├─ VRAM used / total

├─ GPU clock

└─ Fan RPM / duty


### C# Facade Example (ADLXWrapper style)

csharp

using ADLXWrapper;

using var adlx = ADLXApiHelper.Initialize();

using var sys  = adlx.GetSystemServices();

using var perf = sys.GetPerformanceMonitoringServices();

foreach (var gpu in sys.EnumerateGPUs())

{

if (perf.TryGetCurrentGpuMetrics(gpu.UniqueId, out var m))

{

// m.Temperature  (°C)

// m.Usage        (% GPU activity)

// gpu.TotalVRAM  (MB)

// m.ClockSpeed   (MHz)

}

}


### Expected Effect
Returns AMD RDNA/RDNA2/RDNA3 telemetry that RTSS and Adrenalin use internally.

### Example Output Values

text

Temperature = 68.4

Usage       = 91.0

TotalVRAM   = 16384 MB

UsedVRAM    = 11200 MB

ClockSpeed = 2450 MHz


ADLX docs expose support interfaces such as:
- `GetGPUIntakeTemperatureRange()`
- `IADLXGPUMetricsSupport`
- `IPerformanceMonitoring.h` [7](@ref)

---

## 4. Intel — oneAPI Level Zero SysMan (ZES)

### Call Chain

zeInit(ZE_INIT_FLAG_GPU_ONLY)

└─ zeDriverGet()

└─ zeDeviceGet()

└─ cast ze_device_handle_t -> zes_device_handle_t

├─ zesDeviceEnumTemperatureSensors()

│     └─ zesTemperatureGetState()

├─ zesDeviceEnumPowerDomains()

│     └─ zesPowerGetEnergyCounter()

├─ zesDeviceEnumFrequencyDomains()

│     └─ zesFrequencyGetState()

└─ zesDeviceEnumEngineGroups()

└─ zesEngineGetActivity()


### Critical Environment Variable

text

ZES_ENABLE_SYSMAN=1


Without it, SysMan devices/components are not fully initialized. [8](@ref)

### Pseudocode

cpp

setenv("ZES_ENABLE_SYSMAN", "1", 1);

zeInit(ZE_INIT_FLAG_GPU_ONLY);

uint32_t driverCount = 0;

zeDriverGet(&driverCount, nullptr);

ze_driver_handle_t drivers[driverCount];

zeDriverGet(&driverCount, drivers);

for (auto driver : drivers) {

uint32_t devCount = 0;

zeDeviceGet(driver, &devCount, nullptr);

ze_device_handle_t devs[devCount];

zeDeviceGet(driver, &devCount, devs);

for (auto d : devs) {
    zes_device_handle_t sysDev = (zes_device_handle_t)d;

    uint32_t sensorCount = 0;
    zesDeviceEnumTemperatureSensors(sysDev, &sensorCount, nullptr);
    // zesTemperatureGetState(...)
}

}


### Expected Effect
Reads Intel Arc / Intel integrated GPU temperature, power, frequency, and engine busy %.

### Example Output Values

text

GPU temperature = 62.0 °C

GPU frequency   = 2400 MHz

Render engine   = 74% busy

Energy counter  = 1234567 µJ


---

## 5. Cross-Vendor Fallback — LibreHardwareMonitor

### Call Chain

LibreHardwareMonitorLib

└─ Computer { IsGpuEnabled = true }

└─ computer.Open()

└─ hardware.Update()

└─ hardware.Sensors

├─ SensorType.Temperature

├─ SensorType.Load

├─ SensorType.Clock

├─ SensorType.Control  (fan)

└─ SensorType.SmallData/Data (VRAM depending on GPU)


### C# Example

csharp

using LibreHardwareMonitor.Hardware;

var computer = new Computer

{

IsCpuEnabled = false,

IsGpuEnabled = true

};

computer.Open();

foreach (var hw in computer.Hardware)

{

hw.Update();

if (hw.HardwareType == HardwareType.GpuNvidia ||
    hw.HardwareType == HardwareType.GpuAti   ||
    hw.HardwareType == HardwareType.GpuIntel)
{
    foreach (var s in hw.Sensors)
    {
        if (s.SensorType == SensorType.Temperature && s.Name.Contains("Core"))
            Console.WriteLine($"GPU Temp: {s.Value} °C");

        if (s.SensorType == SensorType.Load && s.Name.Contains("GPU Core"))
            Console.WriteLine($"GPU Load: {s.Value} %");

        if (s.SensorType == SensorType.Clock && s.Name.Contains("GPU Core"))
            Console.WriteLine($"GPU Clock: {s.Value} MHz");
    }
}

}


### Notes
- Supports NVIDIA + AMD well.
- Intel support varies by driver.
- Some sensors require **Administrator**.
- VRAM reporting depends on GPU + driver; not always as precise as NVML/ADLX. [9](@ref)

---

## 6. Windows-Native Fallback — DXGI Video Memory

### Call Chain

IDXGIFactory

└─ IDXGIAdapter

└─ QueryInterface(IDXGIAdapter3)

└─ IDXGIAdapter3::QueryVideoMemoryInfo()

├─ DXGI_MEMORY_SEGMENT_GROUP_LOCAL

└─ DXGI_MEMORY_SEGMENT_GROUP_NON_LOCAL


### What You Get
| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `Budget` | Driver/OS memory budget |
| `CurrentUsage` | Bytes currently used |
| `AvailableForReservation` | Reservable bytes |
| `CurrentReservation` | Reserved bytes |

### Limitation
This is **memory accounting**, not temperature or GPU %.
Good for “am I near VRAM budget” logic.

---

## 7. Recommended Dispatch Logic

text

1. Enumerate adapters via DXGI
2. Read adapter description / vendor ID
3. Match vendor:10DE -> NVIDIA -> pynvml / NVML1002 -> AMD    -> ADLX8086 -> Intel  -> Level Zero ZES
4. If backend missing/fails:-> LibreHardwareMonitor
5. For VRAM budget only:-> DXGI QueryVideoMemoryInfo
6. Poll every 1s
7. Push to OSC:/avatar/parameters/GPUTemp      (float)/avatar/parameters/GPUUtil      (float)/avatar/parameters/VRAMUsedGB   (float)/chatbox/input ["GPU 71C 83% VRAM 5.1/8GB", True, False]


---

## 8. OSC Forwarding Example

python

from pythonosc import udp_client

import pynvml, time

pynvml.nvmlInit()

client = udp_client.SimpleUDPClient("127.0.0.1", 9000)

handle = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetHandleByIndex(0)

while True:

temp = pynvml.nvimml  # placeholder

temp = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetTemperature(handle, pynvml.NVML_TEMPERATURE_GPU)

mem  = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetMemoryInfo(handle)

util = pynvml.nvmlDeviceGetUtilizationRates(handle)

client.send_message("/avatar/parameters/GPUTemp", float(temp))
client.send_message("/avatar/parameters/GPUUtil", float(util.gpu))
client.send_message(
    "/avatar/parameters/VRAMUsedGB",
    mem.used / 1024**3
)

client.send_message(
    "/chatbox/input",
    [f"GPU {temp}C {util.gpu}% VRAM {mem.used/1024**3:.1f}/{mem.total/1024**3:.1f}GB", True, False]
)

time.sleep(2)


> Respect chatbox rate limit: max 5 messages / 5 seconds.

---

## 9. Permissions & Pitfalls

| Issue | Cause | Fix |
|-------|-------|-----|
| NVML fails | No NVIDIA driver | Use fallback |
| ADLX returns null | AMD driver not installed | Install Adrenalin |
| ZES returns unsupported | `ZES_ENABLE_SYSMAN` not set | Export env var before init |
| LibreHardwareMonitor missing sensors | Not admin | Run as Administrator |
| VRAM looks wrong | Using DXGI only | Use vendor API for exact used MB |
| Chatbox stops updating | Rate limited | Slow poll to 1.5–2s |

---

## 10. Summary Table

| Data | NVIDIA | AMD | Intel | Fallback |
|------|--------|-----|-------|----------|
| Temperature | ✅ NVML | ✅ ADLX | ✅ ZES | ⚠️ LHM |
| VRAM used | ✅ | ✅ | ⚠️ partial | ⚠️ LHM/DXGI |
| GPU util % | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ engine activity | ⚠️ LHM |
| Power draw | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Fan speed | ✅ | ✅ | ⚠️ | ⚠️ LHM |
| Clocks | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ⚠️ LHM |

**Production answer:** vendor API first, LibreHardwareMonitor second, DXGI third.